WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:06.291
2017's celebrated adaptation of Stephen King's
It is now out on DVD and Blu-ray, and there

00:00:06.291 --> 00:00:09.610
are plenty of deleted and extended scenes
to savor.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:14.410
In all, there are 11 added segments included
in the film's bonus features, so, let's run

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.789
through what's new and how the new clips fit
into the overall story.

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:22.130
And of course, here's the all important spoiler
alert for anyone still waiting to get spooked

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.900
by Pennywise.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.929
Fake out
The first scene included in the bonus features

00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:31.330
is an alternate take of that iconic opening
moment when little Georgie Denbrough chases

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:36.021
his prized paper boat down the rain stream,
and it gets sucked into the sewer drain, where

00:00:36.021 --> 00:00:38.510
Pennywise the Dancing Clown is hiding.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.719
But instead of having his arm ripped off and
being dragged into Derry's dark water, in

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:46.280
this version he simply grabs the boat and
wanders off, saying:

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.680
"See ya later!"

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.059
Pennywise's reaction to Georgie's unexpected
escape?

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:53.140
First, it's silent confusion; then, he finally
utters:

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.200
"Ah s---."

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.020
Dad guilt
Next up, we have the scene with Stanley's

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.329
father chiding him over his lack of preparation
for his bar mitzvah.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:06.229
We already saw him correct his poor pronunciation
and remind his son that he's the rabbi.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:09.470
But in the extended version, he goes for broke
with the guilt trip.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.439
Not only does he essentially tell him he needs
to take responsibility for his soul, but he

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:17.869
also rattles off a list of exactly who he
stands to let down if he messes this up.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:22.070
With that kinda pressure looming on the home
front, it's no wonder Stanley's the most burdened

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.579
of the bunch.

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:26.750
Family friction
One thing that seemed to be a little lacking

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.450
in the theatrical version of It was the emotional
toll that Georgie's disappearance would take

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:32.920
on the rest of the Denbroughs.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.960
In a new scene, though, we find out that not
only are they completely broken, but it's

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.100
Bill who's being inconsiderate and aloof about
everything.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.719
His mom is shown silently laboring over the
dishes, as his father reads a magazine and

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:48.229
Bill starts prattling on about planning their
annual summer park trip.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:52.229
His mother wants nothing to do with it, and
storms off, and that's when his dad informs

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:53.500
him that it's not happening.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.469
This was Georgie's favorite trip, so it doesn't
matter how much everyone else liked it.

00:01:57.469 --> 00:01:58.840
It's done.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:02.270
Second skeptic
Another scene that's extended is the one when

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:07.160
Bill flees from the basement after seeing
a phantom Georgie and Pennywise down below.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.060
"Bill, if you'll come with me, you'll float
too."

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:16.740
He runs into his father upstairs and explains
what he saw, which prompts his father to explore

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.230
the room right away.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:22.150
Even though Bill can clearly hear his dad
sloshing around the flooded basement, his

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:26.390
dad still returns to report that it's "dry
as a bone" and that it must have been a bad

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:27.390
dream.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:31.000
Like other clueless adults around town, his
dad doesn't see or hear what's really going

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.310
on, and thinks it's just a product of Bill's
cruel imagination.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:35.710
Grown ups.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.980
Unhappy home
An aspect of the original It story that's

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:44.370
mercifully glossed over in the cinematic adaptation
is the abuse Henry Bowers suffers at the hands

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.970
of his alcoholic father.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:50.620
In a new scene, though, he's shown struggling
to apply ointment to lashes on his back, implying

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.590
he's been whipped by his mean old man — and
that this isn't the first time.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:58.370
Upon Henry's attempt to the leave the house,
his father demands that he fetch him another

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:02.480
beer first, and he complies without any ounce
hesitation or attitude.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.840
But by the time his friends arrive, Henry's
back to wearing his tough guy air.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.900
"That fat f--- knows if he touches me I'll
rip his head off."

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.000
As they're about to leave, too, they spot
Mike Hanlon passing by on his delivery bike,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.240
which sets up the quick scene with Mike being
bullied by the trio as Pennywise looks on.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.340
Fighting fear
Stanley's emotional defection from the rest

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:27.030
of the Losers is also evident in a new scene
from the Neibolt House, when half the Losers

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.090
play lookout.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.610
Stan cries to Mike about how he doesn't want
to go in the house, and it's clear he just

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.260
can't handle what's happening to all of them
right now.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:38.660
Meanwhile, the scene also sets up a pretty
tender exchange between Ben and Beverly.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:43.010
As they wait for the others, he places a hand
on her shoulder, and she reciprocates by placing

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.760
her hand on top of his own.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.680
An exodus
The next scene is only a slight extension

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.120
of the Neibolt House scene that scared the
hell out of everyone in the theaters.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.340
But in a new piece of the sequence, we get
to see how the group reacts to fighting their

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.370
way out of Pennywise's clutches the first
time.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.440
With Eddie's broken arm, he has to be carted
away on Mike's bike, ditching his own ride

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.220
in the street, and they're all otherwise in
a collective state of panic as they scramble

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:07.490
to flee the scene.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:11.080
Seeing their post-battle fear in action like
this, it's pretty clear why they might be

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:12.810
so quick to disband.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.370
Moody monologue
Stanley Uris might be a shrinking violet throughout

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:20.910
the film, but he gets one scene of serious
retribution in his bar mitzvah monologue.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:25.110
In the extended version of his ceremony, he
lays into his father and the whole synagogue,

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:29.140
by calling them out for being out of touch
with the dangers around town.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:35.380
"Becoming an adult, according to the holy
scripture of Derry, is learning not to give

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:36.820
a s---!"

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:43.520
Perhaps not surprisingly, the only one who
claps for him is Richie "Trashmouth" Tozier.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.910
Cast vandal
In another briefly extended scene, we're finally

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:51.020
shown what Eddie's face looks like when he
realizes that Beverly's bully has signed his

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.640
cast with the word "Loser" down at the pharmacy.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.580
And to make matters worse, we also see her
tack her chewed up wad of gum on top for an

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.280
extra dose of germaphobia.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.130
Slaughter session
The most shocking added scene of them all

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.540
features Henry Bowers, now possessed by Pennywise,
parked outside the Neibolt house with his

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.800
face covered in blood.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.470
He watches as the Losers prepare to go in
and attempt to rescue Beverly from the clown.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.550
"Like lambs to a slaughter."

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.230
That's when the camera pans to the passenger
side and we see that both of his sidekicks

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:23.520
have had their necks slashed, presumably by
Henry's newly reacquired knife.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.810
It's then that the Losers enter the house,
not knowing that they have a twisted Henry

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:27.910
lurking behind them.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:31.710
If included, the scene might've taken away
the element of surprise when Henry shows up

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:36.300
and attacks Mike, but it may have also set
up the eventual excuse Derry folks adopt about

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.040
all the deaths, as they pin them on Henry.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.770
The walkie talkie
In the theatrical version of It, Bill Denbrough

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.950
doesn't get a real sign of his brother's fate
until after they vanquish Pennywise.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:51.280
But in an extended version of the scene, he
stumbles upon Georgie's walkie talkie first

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:52.280
thing.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.060
Considering the condition of the device — it
appears to be unbroken and perhaps even functional

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:00.570
— the find might've given him false hope
about his brother's condition and caused confusion

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:01.740
in the final fight.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.530
So it's probably for the best that the filmmakers
let him have his big showdown with the clown

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.580
before handing him Georgie's old stuff.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.580
Family vacation
The original film ended after the Losers said

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:16.160
goodbye, but the alternative version of the
closing moments would've ended on a slightly

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.889
more optimistic note for at least one character.

00:06:18.889 --> 00:06:23.530
The unused ending included in the bonus features
shows Bill's parents packing up the station

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:27.420
wagon, as his mother comes out to kiss him
and say they'll make new memories with a new

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:28.830
vacation spot.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:32.940
Now that they've all come to accept Georgie's
fate, they're finally starting to heal.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.520
But just to make sure no one gets too cozy,
the camera eventually pans to the storm drain

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.410
that was such a problem for them in the first
place, just as it begins to rain.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:45.690
There's no Pennywise in there — yet — but
it's clear his reign of terror is far from

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:46.690
finished.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:47.690
Thanks for watching!

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:50.250
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.490
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

